= Introduction

Continuity gives us a language for describing change
without losing the structure of what came before.
Consider a system whose state evolves in time, with
each observation contributing to a larger picture.

The central question is not how much a system can do,
but how clearly its behavior can be understood. We
begin with a familiar observation: local rules can
produce surprisingly rich global patterns.

== A local perspective of something

Let $f: RR -> RR$ be a continuous function. For any
point $a$ in its domain, continuity requires that

$ lim_(x -> a) f(x) = f(a). $

This simple condition connects the behavior near a
point to the value at the point itself. It is the
starting point for everything that follows.
